// The look of the tour, as text. `ui.page` writes a whole document and puts
// its own stylesheet in the head of it — that is what makes a `hive.ui`
// server one call rather than an afternoon — and what it writes is a set of
// sensible defaults rather than a design. This file is the design, spliced
// into the same head straight after it, where an equally specific rule wins
// by coming last.
//
// It is layered on rather than replacing anything, and that is deliberate:
// every rule here reaches for a class `hive.ui` already puts on the element
// — `h-link`, `h-column`, `h-bg-good`, `h-spacer` — or for one of the custom
// properties its stylesheet declares. Nothing reaches for a class of its own
// except on the three things the widget set has no answer for, which are the
// same three main.hive writes by hand: a code sample, its caption, and the
// block of prose under them. So a view stays a view, the markup keeps no
// record of having been styled, and dropping this one import puts the tour
// back to stock.
//
// What it is not is a stylesheet for the language. `hive.ui`'s defaults are
// what a program that has not thought about its appearance should look like,
// and they are the right answer to that question. This is one program that
// has thought about it.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What it is trying to look like.
//
// A page can be tidy and say nothing, and the first version of this file was:
// a card here, a hairline there, a rounded amber button that rose a pixel
// under the cursor, everything in the system sans at fifteen pixels, and a
// rail of every page in the site pinned open down the left of every page in
// the site. Every one of those is a good default and not one of them belongs
// to this site in particular — the same stylesheet would have served a
// billing dashboard without a line changed, which is the whole complaint. A
// page that could be about anything ends up looking like it is about nothing.
//
// So this one is about something, and the something is a printed technical
// book. Not a skeuomorph of one: five habits a book has that a web page
// usually does not.
//
//   There is no rail. A book does not carry its table of contents on every
//   page; it has a running head saying what you are reading, a contents page
//   of its own, and a way to turn to the next one. main.hive is where that
//   argument is made in full, because it is an argument about what a tour is
//   rather than about how one looks, and the layout it produces is a view
//   like every other. What is left here is what those three things look like.
//
//   Things are separated by rules and space, never by cards. There is no
//   box-shadow in this file and no element that floats above another. A
//   figure on the page of a book is flush with the paper; so is every sample
//   here.
//
//   The display face is the monospace. This is a page about a programming
//   language: setting its headings, its contents, its labels and its one
//   button in the same face the samples are set in ties the furniture to the
//   subject, and costs nothing — the stack starts at fonts the reader's
//   machine already has, because a site that claims no framework underneath
//   it should not be downloading a typeface either. The prose stays in the
//   sans, because prose is prose.
//
//   Small type is set the way a specimen sheet sets it: monospace, upper
//   case, tracked out wide. A running head, a figure label and a caption all
//   read as apparatus rather than as more sentences.
//
//   Corners are square. A radius is the single most legible mark of the house
//   style being rejected here, and taking it off — from the samples, the
//   screenshots, the button, the focus ring — does more to change what the
//   page looks like than any colour would.
//
// The one ornament is the hexagon, and there is one of it: the bullet against
// each of the claims the front page makes, which is the mark of the language
// reduced to its outline at twenty pixels. Nothing else in here is drawn for
// the sake of drawing.
//
// There was a second one for a while — the same hexagon tiled into a comb,
// washed across the top of every page behind the running head. It was a nice
// piece of work and it was wallpaper. A page that has taken off its shadows,
// its radii and its rail, so that what is left is type and rules and paper,
// does not then want a pattern printed on the paper: every one of those other
// removals was the page getting out of the way of the reading, and the comb
// was the one thing left standing in front of it. A plain ground is not an
// absence of an idea here. It is the idea.
//
// It is held in raw strings, where a brace is a brace and CSS can be written
// the way CSS is written, and split into several of them for the reason the
// tour is split into slides: so each one is about one thing.

// The whole of it, as the single `<style>` the head carries.
func styleTag(): Str {
	return "<style>" + css() + "</style>"
}

// Order matters exactly once in here: `responsive` comes last of the layout
// rules so its media query outranks the frame `shell` sets up.
func css(): Str {
	return palette() + shell() + navigation() + prose() + code() + syntax() + responsive()
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The names ./highlight paints with.
//
// They live here rather than there because a class name is half of a rule,
// and the half that decides what the rule looks like is in this file. The
// painter asks for a name and never spells one, so a name can be changed in
// one place; and there is no way for a span to be emitted with a class this
// stylesheet has no answer for, which is the failure a highlighter usually
// has — markup painted in a colour nobody defined, indistinguishable from
// plain text.
//
// They are one and two characters long because there is one of them around
// every token of every sample on the page. `<span class="k">` against
// `<span class="keyword">` is six bytes, and there are some hundreds of them
// in a slide: the difference is a page a fifth smaller for no cost at all,
// since nothing but this file and its painter ever reads them.

func keywordClass(): Str {
	return "k"
}

func builtinClass(): Str {
	return "b"
}

func typeClass(): Str {
	return "t"
}

func callClass(): Str {
	return "f"
}

func stringClass(): Str {
	return "s"
}

func escapeClass(): Str {
	return "e"
}

func interpClass(): Str {
	return "i"
}

func numberClass(): Str {
	return "n"
}

func atomClass(): Str {
	return "a"
}

func commentClass(): Str {
	return "c"
}

func punctClass(): Str {
	return "p"
}

// The two faces and the colours.
//
// `hive.ui` ships a blue — a good, legible, entirely generic blue — and a
// page about a language whose mark is an amber bee has a better one
// available to it. So the accent becomes the amber, the greys warm by a few
// degrees of hue to sit beside it, and everything downstream of `--accent`
// follows without being told: every link, every focus ring, every border a
// widget draws on its own.
//
// `--brand` is the amber exactly as the drawing paints it. `--accent` is a
// darker mix of the same hue, because the brand amber against paper is barely
// a 2:1 contrast and this one is past 4.5:1. The mark and the prose want the
// same colour and cannot have it, so they get the same hue at two different
// weights. `--rule` is the third name in the family: the page's own ink, for
// the few hairlines that are structure rather than decoration. `--line` stays
// for everything lighter.
//
// `--invert` is the field the button turns into, and it is its own name rather
// than a reuse of `--rule` because the two themes need opposite things from
// it. Inverting means amber text on a dark field, and in the dark theme the
// hairline ink is a mid brown that puts that text at 3.8:1 — under the 4.5
// this page holds itself to everywhere else. Deepening it past the page's own
// background instead gives 9:1, and the light theme's ink already gives 7:1,
// so the one name carries the same idea in both and the contrast is checked
// rather than assumed. That is also why the front page's kicker is `--accent`
// and not `--brand`: small tracked capitals in the drawing's own amber come
// out at 2.2:1 on cream, which is the very trap the two weights exist to
// avoid, and it is an easy one to walk back into.
//
// `--measure` is how wide this site is: the reading column stops there, and
// the running head's padding is written in terms of it so the type in the
// head starts exactly where the prose under it does. One name, because the
// two have to agree and a number written twice is a number that will not.
func palette(): Str {
	return `:root\{
	--f-sans:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,"Segoe UI",Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;
	--f-mono:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,"SF Mono",Menlo,Consolas,"Liberation Mono",monospace;
	--brand:#e29b19;
	--brand-ink:#1f180a;
	--bg:#fdfaf3;
	--fg:#191714;
	--muted:#6b6455;
	--line:#e5dbc5;
	--rule:#231f18;
	--invert:#231f18;
	--accent:#9a5d00;
	--surface:#f7f0df;
	--good:#0d7a55;
	--code:#f4f6f9;
	--code-line:#dde3ea;
	--ring:rgba(226,155,25,.38);
	--measure:1080px;
	--sy-comment:#6f7a8a;
	--sy-punct:#5d6878;
	--sy-keyword:#7038a8;
	--sy-type:#0d6d76;
	--sy-string:#2a7a58;
	--sy-hole:#a03a83;
	--sy-name:#2361ab;
}
@media (prefers-color-scheme:dark)\{:root\{
	--brand:#e5a53a;
	--brand-ink:#17120a;
	--bg:#131210;
	--fg:#ece6d9;
	--muted:#9a9282;
	--line:#2a2621;
	--rule:#554c3f;
	--invert:#0c0b09;
	--accent:#e5a53a;
	--surface:#1a1813;
	--good:#3fbf90;
	--code:#14171c;
	--code-line:#272c34;
	--ring:rgba(229,165,58,.34);
	--sy-comment:#7c8797;
	--sy-punct:#8d96a6;
	--sy-keyword:#c8a4ec;
	--sy-type:#63c4cd;
	--sy-string:#8ccaa6;
	--sy-hole:#e29ac9;
	--sy-name:#8ab6f0;
}}
`
}

// The page itself: the type it is set in, the one column everything sits in,
// and the two things a stylesheet owes a keyboard — a focus ring that is
// visible, and one that is only there for whoever is actually using it.
//
// Every page in this program is now the same shape, which is what taking the
// rail out bought: a wrapper, a running head across the top of it, and one
// column of reading under that. So there is one frame in here instead of two,
// and no rule anywhere in this file has to know whether it is looking at the
// front page or a slide.
//
// The reading column is centred by a `max-width` and a pair of auto margins,
// with `width:100%` written out rather than left to `stretch`. That is not
// belt and braces: an auto margin on the cross axis of a flex container turns
// `stretch` off, and a column that does not stretch is as wide as the longest
// line inside it — which would make every slide a different width. The
// explicit width is what the auto margins then have something to centre.
//
// The running head is full width while the type in it lines up with the
// reading under it, which is one declaration rather than two elements: the
// padding is whatever it takes to reach the reading's own left edge — half
// the leftover room, plus the column's forty — with a floor for the case
// where there is no leftover room at all. So the hairline under it runs the
// whole window and the type above it starts exactly where the prose does.
//
// The wrapper carries no background of its own. `hive.ui` already paints the
// body in `--bg`, and one colour painted once is the whole of what this page
// wants behind it.
//
// The focus ring is square because everything here is, and the offset keeps
// it clear of the thing it is ringing.
func shell(): Str {
	return `html\{-webkit-text-size-adjust:100%}
body\{font:16px/1.7 var(--f-sans);letter-spacing:-.003em;
text-rendering:optimizeLegibility}
::selection\{background:var(--ring);color:var(--fg)}
:focus-visible\{outline:2px solid var(--brand);outline-offset:3px;border-radius:0}
*\{scrollbar-width:thin;scrollbar-color:var(--line) transparent}
::-webkit-scrollbar\{width:10px;height:10px}
::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb\{background:var(--line);
border:3px solid transparent;background-clip:content-box}
::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb:hover\{background:var(--muted);background-clip:content-box}
.h-root>.h-column\{min-height:100vh}
.h-root>.h-column>.h-column\{width:100%;max-width:var(--measure);margin-inline:auto}
.h-root>.h-column>.h-row:first-child\{
padding:15px max(20px,calc((100% - var(--measure)) / 2 + 40px));
border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)}
`
}

// Side by side while there is room for both, and stacked the moment there is
// not. There are only two things on this site that are ever side by side now
// — the front page's two columns and the contents' three — and both are a
// `ui.row`, which is a row inline, in every viewport. There is no attribute
// that says "a row until it is narrow".
//
// That is what the `!important`s are for, and it is the whole of what they
// are for. `ui.row` and `ui.gap(52)` are attributes of a box, and `hive.ui`
// renders an attribute as a `style` attribute — which no rule in a stylesheet
// outranks, however late it is written. So the three properties a phone has
// to take back have to be taken back by force. They sit only in here, and
// only on those three.
//
// The running head wraps rather than being taken apart, because it is four
// things in a line and a line too long for the window is a line that should
// be two. `flex-wrap` is not something any attribute sets, so it costs
// nothing to say here, and it is outside the media query because the right
// width to wrap at is the width where it stops fitting.
//
// The contents is the exception to the thirty. Stacked, its three columns are
// one list again, and a list with thirty pixels every eighteen entries reads
// as three sections of something — which is a grouping the tour does not have
// and the page never meant to claim. Two pixels is the gap the columns
// themselves use, so stacking them puts the list back exactly as it would
// have been written down the page in the first place. Which row is that one
// is a question its contents answer: it is the row whose columns are made of
// links, and `:has` is what asks.
func responsive(): Str {
	return `.h-root>.h-column>.h-row:first-child\{flex-wrap:wrap;row-gap:10px}
@media (max-width:880px)\{
	.h-root>.h-column>.h-column\{padding:26px 20px 52px !important}
	.h-root>.h-column>.h-column>.h-row\{flex-direction:column !important;
	gap:30px !important}
	.h-root>.h-column>.h-column>.h-row:has(>.h-column>.h-link)\{gap:2px !important}
}
`
}

// The running head, the language switch, the bar of progress, the contents,
// and the pager at the foot of a slide. All of it is navigation, and all of
// it is the same argument: `hive.ui` renders a link as a link, which is the
// right default and the wrong thing for a line of apparatus across the top of
// a page — at that size and that count the blue stops being a signal and
// becomes the decoration.
//
// So the head is set as a running head: small, monospace, upper case, tracked
// out, in the page's own ink, earning the accent by being hovered. Four items
// with a `ui.spacer` between the second and the third, and the spacer needs
// no rule at all — `flex:1 1 auto` is what the widget already renders as, and
// it is exactly right.
//
// The progress bar is two boxes in a row and the ratio between them, so all
// this has to say is what the filled half is painted in. `h-bg-good` is the
// class `ui.background(Tone.Good())` renders as, and `hive.ui`'s own
// stylesheet paints it green; the tour paints it amber, in the one place that
// class lands on a box rather than on the button. The empty half is left
// alone, because the hairline under the head is already the track, and a
// second one drawn over it would only be darker in places.
//
// The contents is set as the index of a book, which is what it is. Monospace,
// so the numbers down the left edge line up into a column of their own; and a
// hanging indent of exactly four characters, so a title long enough to wrap
// comes back underneath the title rather than underneath the number. Four is
// not a guess — main.hive pads the number to two digits precisely so that
// every entry reads `NN. ` and every one of them is four characters wide. The
// two files have to agree about that, and the comment in each of them says so.
//
// The language switch used to be a bordered pill with the current half filled
// in, which is a segmented control, which is a widget out of an application
// and not a thing the page of a book contains. Now it is two words with a
// rule under the one you are reading in. It is marked with
// `ui.background(Tone.Muted())` and that class is what the rule hangs on, so
// the view still describes the state and this file still decides what the
// state looks like. The `background:none` is not tidiness: the half that is
// *not* current is `Tone.Normal()`, which `hive.ui` renders as
// `background:var(--bg)`, and a fill of the page's own colour is a fill that
// shows up the moment what is under it is not the page.
func navigation(): Str {
	return `.h-root>.h-column>.h-row:first-child>.h-link\{
background:none;color:var(--fg);font:600 11px/1.4 var(--f-mono);
letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;transition:color .12s ease}
.h-root>.h-column>.h-row:first-child>.h-link:hover\{
color:var(--accent);text-decoration:none}
.h-root>.h-column>.h-row:first-child>.h-row\{gap:14px !important}
.h-root>.h-column>.h-row:first-child>.h-row>.h-link\{
padding:0 0 3px;background:none;font:700 11px/1 var(--f-mono);
letter-spacing:.14em;color:var(--muted);
border-bottom:1px solid transparent}
.h-root>.h-column>.h-row:first-child>.h-row>.h-link:hover\{
color:var(--accent);text-decoration:none}
.h-root>.h-column>.h-row:first-child>.h-row>.h-link.h-bg-muted\{
background:none;color:var(--fg);border-bottom-color:var(--brand)}
.h-root>.h-column>.h-row:nth-child(2)\{margin-top:-1px}
.h-root>.h-column>.h-row>.h-column.h-bg-good\{background:var(--brand)}
.h-root>.h-column>.h-column>.h-row>.h-column>.h-link\{
display:block;background:none;color:var(--fg);
font:12.5px/1.55 var(--f-mono);letter-spacing:-.01em;
padding:3px 6px 3px calc(6px + 4ch);text-indent:-4ch;
transition:color .12s ease,background .12s ease}
.h-root>.h-column>.h-column>.h-row>.h-column>.h-link:hover\{
background:var(--surface);color:var(--accent);text-decoration:none}
.tour-prose>.h-column>.h-row:last-child\{
margin-top:16px;padding-top:18px;border-top:1px solid var(--line)}
.tour-prose>.h-column>.h-row:last-child .h-link,
.tour-prose>.h-column>.h-row:last-child .h-text\{
font:600 12px/1 var(--f-mono);letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase}
.tour-prose>.h-column>.h-row:last-child .h-text\{color:var(--muted)}
`
}

// The reading: the headings, the paragraphs, the list of what the language
// is, and the one button on the site.
//
// The headings are monospace, and that is the single decision this file turns
// on. A title set in the same face as the code beneath it is a page that has
// admitted what it is about; the same title in the system sans is a page that
// could be selling anything. The front page takes it furthest — the name of
// the language at sixty-odd pixels, tracked in tight, beside the mark — and
// every page after it carries the smaller version of the same idea, over a
// rule, under a line saying which of the fifty-four this is.
//
// The front page's title is told apart from every other title by where it
// sits rather than by a class of its own. `.h-image + .h-column > h1` is a
// drawing with a heading beside it, and the only place on the site where that
// is true is the masthead — a slide's screenshot is inside a column with its
// caption under it, never beside a heading. So the view says "a picture and a
// title in a row", the stylesheet reads that back, and neither of them has to
// carry a name for the front page.
//
// The claims on the front page used to be check marks, which is the bullet
// every landing page has used for fifteen years. They are hexagons now: the
// same `ui.icon` view, the same tick inside it, in a tile clipped to the
// shape of the mark. Six of them down the right of the front page is the only
// place the shape appears at all, now that the background is a plain one.
//
// The button is the rule this whole file was worth writing for. `hive.ui`
// renders `ui.link([ui.background(Tone.Good())], ...)` as an anchor carrying
// both `h-link` and `h-bg-good`, and its own stylesheet sets a colour in each
// of them — the green fill in one, the accent text in the other — with the
// link rule written second. So the two disagree, the later one wins, and the
// call that plainly means "this is the button" comes out as blue text on a
// green field with no padding at all. One rule naming both classes at once
// settles it.
//
// What it settles into is amber under the page's own ink, square, with no
// shadow and nothing that moves. It used to rise a pixel and grow a soft
// shadow on hover, which is the gesture every button on the web makes and
// which says only that the cursor is where the cursor plainly is. This one
// inverts instead — ink where the amber was, amber where the ink was — which
// is a thing ink on paper can actually do, and which is unmistakable at a
// glance rather than a pixel of parallax you have to be looking for.
func prose(): Str {
	return `h1.h-size-title\{font:700 33px/1.15 var(--f-mono);
letter-spacing:-.035em;margin:0}
.h-root>.h-column>.h-column>.h-column:first-child\{
padding-bottom:18px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)}
.h-image+.h-column>h1.h-size-title\{
font-size:clamp(40px,7vw,58px);line-height:1;letter-spacing:-.045em}
.h-image+.h-column>.h-size-caption\{color:var(--accent)}
.h-size-subtitle\{letter-spacing:-.012em}
.h-size-caption\{font:600 11px/1.4 var(--f-mono);letter-spacing:.16em;
text-transform:uppercase}
.h-column>.h-text\{max-width:66ch}
.h-link\{transition:color .12s ease}

/* The claims on the front page: the tick ui.icon writes, in a hexagon. The
   view asks for an icon and a tone and gets both — nothing here is spliced,
   and taking this one rule out puts the plain check mark back. A CSS comment
   inside a raw string is one of the few places on this page that cannot spell
   a name in backticks, because a backtick is what ends the string. */
.h-icon.h-tone-good\{display:inline-grid;place-items:center;
width:20px;height:22px;flex:0 0 auto;margin-top:3px;font-size:11px;line-height:1;
background:var(--brand);color:var(--brand-ink);
clip-path:polygon(50% 0,100% 25%,100% 75%,50% 100%,0 75%,0 25%)}
.h-image\{filter:none}

/* A slide's screenshot. It is a picture of a window, so it is given the edge
   a window has — the same hairline the code samples carry, since both are the
   same kind of thing on the page: a figure holding something the prose is
   talking about rather than saying. Which is also why the amber rule came off
   this at the same time it came off those: the whole point of the two being
   dressed alike is that they go on being dressed alike. Square, flush, and no
   shadow, because nothing on this page floats. */
.h-root>.h-column>.h-column .h-column>.h-image\{
width:100%;height:auto;border:1px solid var(--code-line);
background:var(--code)}
.h-link.h-bg-good\{
display:inline-block;background:var(--brand);color:var(--brand-ink);
font:700 12px/1 var(--f-mono);letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;
padding:13px 20px;border:1px solid var(--brand);
transition:background .13s ease,color .13s ease,border-color .13s ease}
.h-link.h-bg-good:hover\{
background:var(--invert);color:var(--brand);border-color:var(--invert);
text-decoration:none}
.h-link.h-bg-good:active\{
background:var(--accent);color:var(--bg);border-color:var(--accent)}
`
}

// The code samples, which are the only part of the page that is not a view
// and never could be: `hive.ui` has no monospaced, boxed text, so main.hive
// writes the `<pre>` by hand. What it writes carries a class and no style
// attribute, and the style is here — which is the whole reason the class
// exists, since a `style=` written inline in the middle of a Str is a thing
// nobody can find again later.
//
// A sample is a figure, and it is set the way a book sets one: a label above
// it in small tracked capitals, and a panel flush with the text below it. A
// hairline and nothing else — no radius, no shadow, and no rule down the left
// edge. There was an amber one there for a while, on the reasoning that a
// panel this cold in the middle of a page this warm wanted something of the
// page's colour touching it. It did not: the sample already announces itself
// twice over, by its fill and by every token inside it, and a third mark on
// the edge was the page pointing at something that was not hiding. The panel
// scrolls sideways on its own rather than widening the column, because a long
// line in one example is not a reason for every paragraph on the page to get
// longer.
func code(): Str {
	return `.tour-example\{margin:0 0 26px}
.tour-example:last-child\{margin-bottom:0}
.tour-caption\{margin:0 0 8px;font:600 11px/1.4 var(--f-mono);
letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted)}
.tour-code\{margin:0;padding:16px 18px;overflow-x:auto;tab-size:4;
background:var(--code);border:1px solid var(--code-line)}
.tour-code code\{font:13.5px/1.65 var(--f-mono);font-variant-ligatures:none;
letter-spacing:0;color:var(--fg)}
.tour-prose\{display:flex;flex-direction:column}
`
}

// What ./highlight's spans look like. Every rule is scoped under `.tour-code`,
// so a one-letter class name can never reach anything but a code sample.
//
// Every one of them is cold, and that is the point of them. The page around
// the samples is amber and warm greys all the way down — it is a site about a
// bee — and a code theme in the same family would have the samples melting
// into the paper they sit on. So the panel turns a few degrees blue, and
// everything written on it is violet, teal, green, mauve and azure: nothing
// on this list shares a hue with anything outside the box it is in. The
// contrast is what says *this is not prose, this is a program* before a word
// of it has been read, and it does that work at a glance and from across the
// room — which matters more now than it did, because the headings are
// monospace too, and the face is no longer the thing telling them apart.
//
// Five hues and a grey, which is fewer than a code theme usually spends, and
// the grouping is by what a thing *is* rather than by what it is called. An
// escape and an interpolation share a colour because they are the same idea
// twice — a hole in a string where something that is not text goes — and
// seeing them as one thing is more use than telling them apart. A builtin and
// a call share one because both are something being called; the builtin
// carries the weight, because it is the one a reader may not recognise.
//
// The comment is the only rule that takes light from the page rather than
// adding to it. A comment in these samples is not an aside — half the tour's
// explanation lives in them — so it recedes rather than disappears, and it is
// the one token deliberately kept above the contrast a body of grey text
// would need.
func syntax(): Str {
	return `.tour-code .c\{color:var(--sy-comment)}
.tour-code .p\{color:var(--sy-punct)}
.tour-code .k\{color:var(--sy-keyword)}
.tour-code .t\{color:var(--sy-type)}
.tour-code .s\{color:var(--sy-string)}
.tour-code .n\{color:var(--sy-hole)}
.tour-code .a\{color:var(--sy-hole)}
.tour-code .e\{color:var(--sy-hole)}
.tour-code .i\{color:var(--sy-hole)}
.tour-code .f\{color:var(--sy-name)}
.tour-code .b\{color:var(--sy-name);font-weight:600}
`
}
